WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.377
CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW lets you update a compatible view definition without first dropping the object.

00:00:06.377 --> 00:00:12.722
This is useful when the interface should remain available under the same name.

00:00:12.722 --> 00:00:18.827
But compatibility matters. Changing column names, meanings, types, or grain can still affect downstream users,

00:00:18.827 --> 00:00:21.352
even if the SQL command succeeds.

00:00:21.352 --> 00:00:25.957
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:25.957 --> 00:00:32.102
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:32.102 --> 00:00:34.627
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:34.627 --> 00:00:41.107
In a real setting such as an analytics team exposing a stable reporting view to dashboard users,

00:00:41.107 --> 00:00:47.042
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:47.042 --> 00:00:49.232
before discussing syntax.

00:00:49.232 --> 00:00:55.331
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
